plumbing: revlist, Count stale entries in the paint walk

The painted commit walk in `Objects` decided whether to stop by
scanning its priority queue after every pop, until it found an entry
that the wants and the haves had not both painted. When many stale
commits are queued ahead of one that is not, for example when one side
merges a commit older than a wide stretch of shared history, every pop
rescans them, and the walk turns quadratic in the width of that
history.

Move the queue and its paint flags into a `paintQueue` that counts the
queued entries that are not yet stale. Pushing and popping adjust the
count by the entry's current flags, and a commit turning stale while
queued drops all its queued entries from it at once, so the walk stops
when the count reaches zero without rescanning the queue.

Git made the same change to the painted walk that finds merge bases,
`paint_down_to_common()`: a186b7797a [1] replaced the scan in
`queue_has_nonstale()` with a pointer to the oldest commit that is not
stale, and a3e5bad734 [2] replaced the pointer with counts of the
queued commits that are not stale [3]. Git queues a commit at most
once, while here a commit is queued again when its flags change, so
the queue also keeps a count of entries per commit.

In `BenchmarkObjectsWideHistory`, `Objects` takes 95% less time with
1000 branches (403.3ms to 20.2ms) and 65% less with 100 (5.70ms to
1.98ms), for about 3% more memory. `BenchmarkObjects` does not change.
Both are benchstat over 10 alternating runs.

